17:34:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've made an excellent and very sharp point. You're correct: the body can and does make glucose from protein through **gluconeogenesis** (GNG). So technically, a person could eat zero carbohydrates and still supply the brain with the glucose it needs, as long as they eat enough protein (and fat for energy). This is the basis of very low-carb, ketogenic diets. So, if GNG solves the brain's glucose requirement, **does that mean the source of your food truly doesn't matter for energy?** Not quite. Here’s why the distinction still matters, even with GNG in the picture. ### 1. Gluconeogenesis Is an "Emergency" Pathway, Not a Preferred One While GNG is always running at a low level, ramping it up to fully replace dietary carbs is like using a backup generator 24/7 instead of the main power grid. - **It's energetically expensive.** Producing glucose from amino acids requires ATP. The process consumes more energy than it gives in the final glucose molecule. If you eat 100 calories of protein and your body converts some of it to glucose, you get *less* net energy than if you had eaten 100 calories of carbohydrate directly. You're losing energy just to make the fuel. - **It produces waste.** The nitrogen from the amino acids must be removed and converted to urea, which taxes the kidneys and requires water. It's not a clean conversion. ### 2. Protein Has a "Job" — You Don't Want to Burn It for Fuel This is the critical practical point. Amino acids are the building blocks for your muscles, enzymes, hormones, immune cells, and neurotransmitters. If you constantly rely on gluconeogenesis to make glucose, you're diverting those amino acids away from their primary structural and regulatory roles. - **Imagine your body as a house.** The wood (protein) is used for the walls and roof. If you start burning the walls to keep warm (make glucose), the house gets weaker. Your body will break down its own muscle tissue if dietary protein is insufficient to meet