ories on a high-protein diet because satiety kicks in earlier, even if the protein is less efficient. This is why high-protein diets are often associated with weight loss—not because protein doesn't provide energy, but because the body stops you from eating enough to get the same net energy. ### The Brain's "Energy Tax" Is Not Just About Quantity Even though gluconeogenesis can supply glucose to the brain, it does so at a **slower and more regulated rate** than dietary carbs. The brain uses about 120 grams of glucose per day. If you get all of that from protein, you need to consume about 200–250 grams of protein (since ~50–60% of ingested protein can be converted to glucose, depending on other factors). That's a lot of protein for a single day—around 1 kg of lean chicken or steak. But here's the twist: *Your liver cannot ramp up gluconeogenesis instantly*. If you skip breakfast, your blood glucose drops, and your brain depends on stored liver glycogen. After about 12–18 hours of fasting, glycogen is depleted, and GNG takes over fully. During that transition, many people experience brain fog, fatigue, and irritability—not because they lack total energy, but because the *timing* of energy delivery is mismatched with demand. So the **source matters for the *temporal availability* of energy**, even if the total ATP over 24 hours is the same. Your body's regulatory systems (insulin, glucagon, cortisol) are tuned to handle different fuels at different rates. If you rely solely on GNG, you lose the rapid-response capability that dietary carbs provide. ### The Metabolic "Gearbox" Analogy Think of your body as a car with a manual transmission. - Carbohydrates are like first gear: high revs, quick acceleration, good for bursts. - Fats are like fifth gear: efficient cruising, poor for sudden speed. - Protein is like shifting into neutral and then into gear again: it works, but you waste time and energy in the transition. You can drive the car in any gear, but the *experience* 